{"assets":["/editorial-assets/dba48cc5cf321b22f9dba0072b75f1446cba365f2f95aa40c9ce2b926aa4f521.png","/editorial-assets/52a5f2a5cb09c6e01c9066fb2559453c109888e718f229d8757d54541d661d43.png","/editorial-assets/ede6245292bc9a873770468a569284cb407892080f347b341f86e6a248837d02.png","/editorial-assets/ad58d78a728c2206be1ff6c3306e5ba7cc7baa83075b28f3882778b72c677b32.png","/editorial-assets/ca9c4bebff0fce98f63a71ec5c47568731de306475a4881b68e22784760d90a8.png","/editorial-assets/476c7594b87b9298d3bec7efe9e7f8f431c68685da0e24489e5a00c149f10984.png","/editorial-assets/168ce26dcbb9c8b9a1ab78008c19b3881e4840ab2826794af21ea638894762d5.png","/editorial-assets/2b900c1b45ee87ca20dd43d72966de4d62a86a120afc2b4bf72645a4d58829ff.png","/editorial-assets/105d20befc6251b3ea99236d5c1ae40a45f142dfc058945ee951660f2d51127b.png","/editorial-assets/7881eca8912a68cf2ca3dc9cedec0e3e839128fcdcc5ada8dcc2792b973c9e92.png"],"contestId":"755","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"8VC Venture Cup 2017 — Elimination Round[Editorial]"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"755A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/755/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"755A - Польшар и Гипотеза"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There are many ways to solve the problem. There can't be many primes in a row, so we can just bruteforce until we find a composite number. Note that it is not neccesarily true for huge numbers. More general, there is a prime arithmetic progression of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for any "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", but there is no infinitely-long sequence!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Another way to solve the problem is noticing that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 2) + 1 = ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1)·("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1), so we can just output "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 2. However, we can't do that when "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 2. This is a special case."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"755B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/755/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"755B - Польшар и Игра"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let PolandBall know "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" words, EnemyBall "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" words. Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be set of words which both Balls know. It's easy to see that Balls should process words from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" first. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" contains even number of words, PolandBall will have an advantage of one additional word. Then, if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", PolandBall loses. Otherwise - he wins."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"You may wonder what is the strange kremowka thing in the samples. It's a probably most famous Polish... cake. Like this:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/dba48cc5cf321b22f9dba0072b75f1446cba365f2f95aa40c9ce2b926aa4f521.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Its fame originated when our Polish Pope, John Paul II said: \"After the exams, we were eating kremówki\". Delicious."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"755C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/755/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"755C - Польшар и Лес"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Property: Look at one tree and take its diameter. Name its endpoints A and B. For each vertex u from this component, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" or "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". It's easy to prove that."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can just count number of different elements in P and divide it by two."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Special case : Isolated vertices (those with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Poland has a diverse landscape. We have forests (PolandBall had to live somewhere =) ), mountains, sea and even deserts. We have all 4 weather seasons. And various beautiful animals live here. Like żubr, King of The Wilderness. Say hi!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/52a5f2a5cb09c6e01c9066fb2559453c109888e718f229d8757d54541d661d43.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"755D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/755/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"755D - Польшар и Многоугольник"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First, we can set "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). This changes nothing right now, but we'll use it later. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Our diagonals correspond to intervals ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"R"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"], each intersection of two diagonals:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A) adds one to result"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B) is equivalent to intersection of their intervals"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We will use segment or fenwick tree to store beginnings and ends of intervals. Because of the fact that all the segments have equal length (length = K) we know exactly what their intersection looks like."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Also, in order to maintain edges like (7, 3) we'll insert some intervals twice. Because of changes we did to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the very beginning, we'll never count anything more than once."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Complexity : "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"NlogN"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"755E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/755/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"755E - Польшар и Бело-красный граф"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We should definitely start with estimating how big can be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". It turns out that we can't obtain diameter greater than 3. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"You can prove this fact by yourself or try here: "},{"attrs":{"href":"http://math.stackexchange.com/questions/1929512/if-the-diameter-of-graph-is-greater-than-3-then-the-diameter-of-its-complement-g"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Proof."}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can also easily show that answer is  - 1 for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"So, the problem is to solve K = 2 and K = 3."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Constructing K = 3:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's start with graph G, which is the smallest possible graph:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1 - 2"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2 - 3"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3 - 4"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We will add vertices <5.."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"> one by one, when vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" will be joint with vertices 1 and 3, and if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > 5, with vertices <5; "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1>."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It's easy to see that ShortestPath(1, 4) is exactly 3. Also, ShortestPath(1, 4) in the complement graph is 3. Because of the property, this is a valid solution for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 3."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Constructing K = 2:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"When "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 4, answer is  - 1."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Graph G : path from 1 to N."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"G has diameter N - 1, but it does not matter. The smaller one is from complement graph. It is easy to see that for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > 4 the diameter of the second graph is exactly 2."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Of course there are many other ways to construct both graphs. Note that both red and white graphs have to be connected and we should pay some attention to it."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Someone in the comments before the round said: \"Give him a hat, and he will become IndonesiaBall. \" Indeed, it's true. Country Balls usually have colours the same as flags of countries they represent. However, when PolandBall originated the creator mistakenly drew it as red-white instead of white-red. PolandBall was mad, but decided to stay this way =)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ede6245292bc9a873770468a569284cb407892080f347b341f86e6a248837d02.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This is a picture of a PolandBall dressed as husar. The husars were a Polish military formation from XVII century, a very powerful one. Usually recognizable because of the special wings."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"755F"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/755/problem/F"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"755F - Польшар и Подарки"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Every permutation can be represented as a set of cycles."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Maximum:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This is the easier case. Greedy works, some careful implementation and it's ok. Answer is usually 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in this case, but not always."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Minimum:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If there are such cycles "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ... "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + ... + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"K"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then K it's enough. Otherwise the result can be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"K"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We would like to use knapsack to check it. However, the constraints are too big in this case for a normal knapsack."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We will choose some parameter "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and process all numbers bigger than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" normally. If there are "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such elements, then complexity of this step is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ad58d78a728c2206be1ff6c3306e5ba7cc7baa83075b28f3882778b72c677b32.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". There can be at most "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ca9c4bebff0fce98f63a71ec5c47568731de306475a4881b68e22784760d90a8.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" numbers bigger than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", so it's "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/476c7594b87b9298d3bec7efe9e7f8f431c68685da0e24489e5a00c149f10984.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"What should we do with numbers smaller than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"? We'll process all numbers of the same type at once. When processing number of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we will maintain array "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"[] which denotes \"how many numbers of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are neccessary to have a certain number knapsack-possible. This step works in O("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Complexity : O("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/168ce26dcbb9c8b9a1ab78008c19b3881e4840ab2826794af21ea638894762d5.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"). We should choose such "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that this value is minimum possible. "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 100 seems reasonable."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The situation described in a problem is a possible one. There is a tradition in Poland that we give our friends or schoolmates gifts during Christmas. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/2b900c1b45ee87ca20dd43d72966de4d62a86a120afc2b4bf72645a4d58829ff.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"755G"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/755/problem/G"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"755G - PolandBall and Many Other Balls"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The modulo instantly suggests an FFT-like approach. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First, let's solve this problem using simple dynamic programming."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"DP[N][K] = DP[N-1][K] + DP[N-1][K-1] + DP[N-2][K-1], because we can either do nothing or add one interval of length 1 or 2. As for start, this works fine. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can calculate DP[1], DP[2], DP[3] and DP[4] using this simple formula. Calculating DP[x] means calculating DP[x][k] for all values k in range <1, K>, where K is the number from statement."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We will perform binary exponentation of DPs. In order to do so, we should be able to count DP[x + 1] and DP[2 * x]. If we could do this operations, then we can obtain the result in O(logN)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Important property: DP[a + b] can be counted using DP[a] * DP[b] and DP[a - 1] * DP[b - 1], when we treat DP[x] as polynomial with coefficients DP[x][0], DP[x][1], .... It is based by the fact that we can divide the sequence of length (a + b) in two parts of length a and b."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If there is no segment of length 2 starting from position a, then we can choose some segments from the first part, and the remaining segments from the second part. For fixed number of intervals k, this is a sum of DP[a][i] * DP[b][k - i]. So, this is convolution of polynomials DP[A] and DP[B]."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"However, there may also be such a segment. In this case, everything stays the same, but we have k - 1 intervals instead of k and convolution of DP[a - 1] and DP[b - 1]. So, with some calculations we can count DP[a + b] using those DPs."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Assume we have DP[n], DP[n - 1] and DP[n - 2]. Now we are trying to count DP[2 * n], DP[2 * n - 1], DP[2 * n - 2]."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"DP[2 * n] = DP[n + n], which can be counted using multiplication of DP[n] and DP[n-1]."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"DP[2*n-1] = DP[n + (n-1)], which can be counted using multiplication of DP[n], DP[n-1] and DP[n-2]."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"DP[2*n-2] = DP[(n-1) + (n-1)], which can be counted using multiplication of DP[n-1] and DP[n-2]."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Therefore, we can obtain another, two times bigger, triplet of DPs. Of course, having DP[n], DP[n-1] and DP[n-2] in memory we can also easily count DP[n + 1] in O(K)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can also calculate DP[2*n], DP[2*n-1] and DP[2*n-2] in O(K log K). This is enough to perform the binary exponentation."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Overall solution works in O(K log N log K). "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Statement says \"Balls are proud of their home land - and they want to prove that it's strong.\" It originated, of course, from Independence Marches. On November 11th, Poland celebrates an anniversary of getting independence in year 1918, after the World War I and 123 years of enslavement. Those Marches are spectacular."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/105d20befc6251b3ea99236d5c1ae40a45f142dfc058945ee951660f2d51127b.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Unfortunately, this is the end of your meeting with PolandBall. I hope that you liked the problems. Good luck during next contests. I hope to see you soon! Any comments, especially with feedback are really appreciated."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Have a nice day!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" PolandBall"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7881eca8912a68cf2ca3dc9cedec0e3e839128fcdcc5ada8dcc2792b973c9e92.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/49793"}